This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,

$$
\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{u^2+1}}\,\textrm{d}u = \operatorname{arsinh}  u+C.
$$

The only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \left(\sqrt{2}\,x\right)^2.$

So, let's substitute $u =\sqrt{2}\,x.$ Differentiating, we get

$$
\dfrac{\textrm{d}u}{\textrm{d}x}=\sqrt{2}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\textrm{d}x = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \,\textrm{d}u.
$$

Using the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}\begin{aligned}\int \frac{\pi }{\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\,\text{d}x & =\int \frac{\pi }{\sqrt{{(\sqrt{2}\,x)}^{2}+1}}\,\text{d}x \\  & =\pi \int \frac{1}{\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\,\cdot (\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\,\text{d}u) \\  & =\frac{\pi }{\sqrt{2}}\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\,\text{d}u \\  & =\frac{\pi }{\sqrt{2}}\text{\arsinh}u+C \\  & =\frac{\pi }{\sqrt{2}}\text{\arsinh}(\sqrt{2}\,x)+C\end{aligned}\end{aligned}
$$

